#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486512} :activity/id #uuid "123b57a8-59a0-5d9a-805f-fed4b8283678" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/id #uuid "bd333eb5-72e6-5eaa-be12-c6bf36306b90" :topic/math-academy-id 1910} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e5944a2-6f72-5f21-8b24-e64767793229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486018} {:answer/id #uuid "9f66dc53-0b31-5237-969a-2397bba21b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486050} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2979c6-49e8-5225-824c-1a6f38548151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486078} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffe2c5-8479-52b3-b64d-d795dd8a896a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486084} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cc0c71-6572-5be7-b79e-f4264330ac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486018} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71b80ef-6d4c-597c-814a-17f8214907ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72bf6680-369b-5d8c-9e49-1546d6428898" :question/math-academy-id "q-113598" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "50c856b2-5de1-5fc6-b8b6-216f5affc71d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277d51c6-ba77-5bde-a9da-6f2d8333899a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348786} {:answer/id #uuid "36849b8b-2c62-5eb1-bb53-ebb31890e317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348938} {:answer/id #uuid "526976b5-686a-54a2-a200-041c5d48d668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349197} {:answer/id #uuid "8056b724-3039-56ac-ac48-651200dd450e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349623} {:answer/id #uuid "9abeb6bd-73c2-5d7d-9344-2f69c899f0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348938} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8cbbee3-2e8a-56b1-9089-55eda08012bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49aa77db-aeb3-5225-bb69-221318751e9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82718" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b02f895b-b4b9-567f-99da-8866008266c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b3fc777-c87e-5494-9361-753fc99b8a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417145} {:answer/id #uuid "af2e1656-5790-518b-ae95-17ddec42b6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417157} {:answer/id #uuid "c820d187-6507-5a27-8119-ca85d381bd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417165} {:answer/id #uuid "d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417166} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368894-a420-5e78-a8ce-445dfc038e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417166} :answer-field/id #uuid "77968fba-758e-5c47-b717-abe8d60fbc59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "618e1711-d591-5872-b815-9d14ad33b674" :question/math-academy-id "q-37160" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 16-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cea4b00d-cc11-568c-8bd2-36b2db4582ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1696c49c-b8ba-5c9f-a1e2-a234d7a12381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485804} {:answer/id #uuid "33eeaaae-80c9-51a7-944e-f618a820b3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485862} {:answer/id #uuid "35031deb-a4ba-5495-89d6-18fe7aca9f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485864} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2528e3-8cd1-5c53-9f19-daeb351a37d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486101} {:answer/id #uuid "e673df76-6d49-52f2-85e7-cffc12218f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486101} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0731c0a-4746-54da-b38a-46b9b2e964b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae7fdbe5-ac31-5cc5-8e79-33235643a822" :question/math-academy-id "q-36502" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 10-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7f877e0-9517-58da-83be-55a89211a009" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15690e8b-c556-578a-a848-9e2012319066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485801} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce04a03-a227-57a9-bb1f-9d7b6996fb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485848} {:answer/id #uuid "972d0aed-9872-5a09-9680-0c04b590dfee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486037} {:answer/id #uuid "b85661ff-f141-5e76-a9a5-e29f75d6b6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486097} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bcf6bc-21ea-5ab1-b19b-a0bda85a2f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486097} :answer-field/id #uuid "f917e741-700b-51ed-9e8c-db10a05c79b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd3d1f8f-1ec9-5277-bd94-fc774bb919de" :question/math-academy-id "q-113595" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7ff6d11-7ab8-5df7-ab96-cc2f9b5a933c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486482}}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485749}]}
